## 10. How to Add a New Test Case

No Python changes are needed. Only SQL is required.

### Step 1 — Open (or create) a `.sql` file

```
tests/sql/testcases/test_usp_delta_folders.sql   ← add FOLDERS tests here
tests/sql/testcases/test_usp_delta_master.sql    ← add MASTER tests here
tests/sql/testcases/test_usp_my_new_sp.sql       ← or create a new file
```

### Step 2 — Write the test block

```sql
-- @@TEST: TC22 - My new scenario name
-- @@DESCRIPTION: One sentence explaining what this test verifies

-- Setup: any INSERT / DECLARE statements needed
DECLARE @staging_id BIGINT;
INSERT INTO dbo.STAGING DEFAULT VALUES;
SET @staging_id = SCOPE_IDENTITY();

INSERT INTO dbo.STAGING_FOLDERS
    (folderIfUnmodifiedSince, folderFolderID, folderStateCode,
     folderTypeCode, folderDescription, folderOwnerEntityId, staging_id, Is_Processed)
VALUES (NULL, 'MY-FOLDER', 'ACTIVE', 'TYPE-A', 'My Description', 'ENTITY-001', @staging_id, 'N');

-- Call the stored procedure under test
EXEC dbo.USP_DELTA_FOLDERS;

-- @@ASSERT: Description of the first check
SELECT CASE WHEN COUNT(*) = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
FROM dbo.TARGET_FOLDERS;

-- @@ASSERT: Description of the second check
SELECT CASE WHEN status = 'SUCCESS' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
FROM dbo.SYS_DELTA_LOG
ORDER BY log_id DESC OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROW ONLY;

-- @@END_TEST
```

### Step 3 — Run pytest

```bash
pytest tests/ -v
```

Your new test is discovered and executed automatically.

---

## 11. Assertion Contract

Every `@@ASSERT` SELECT must conform to the following contract:

| Return value | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `1` (integer) | Assertion **passes** |
| `0` | Assertion **fails** |
| `NULL` | Assertion **fails** — treated as non-1 value |
| No rows returned | Assertion **fails** — "returned no rows" error |
| Multiple columns | Only the **first column** of the first row is evaluated |
| Multiple rows | Only the **first row** is evaluated |

**Pattern for count checks:**
```sql
SELECT CASE WHEN COUNT(*) = 3 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
FROM dbo.TARGET_FOLDERS;
```

**Pattern for value checks:**
```sql
SELECT CASE WHEN status = 'SUCCESS' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
FROM dbo.SYS_DELTA_LOG
ORDER BY log_id DESC OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROW ONLY;
```

**Pattern for NULL checks:**
```sql
SELECT CASE WHEN duration_seconds IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
FROM dbo.SYS_DELTA_LOG
ORDER BY log_id DESC OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROW ONLY;
```

---

## 12. Failure Message Format

When one or more assertions fail, pytest reports a combined message:

```
FAILED tests/test_sql_runner.py::test_sql_case[test_usp_delta_folders__TC07_...]
AssertionError: Test 'TC07 - All metrics captured ...' – 2 assertion(s) failed:
  [1] Assertion 4 'rows_deduped is 1' FAILED (got 0, expected 1).
      SQL: SELECT CASE WHEN rows_deduped = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
           FROM dbo.SYS_DELTA_LOG ORDER BY log_id DESC OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROW ONLY;
  [2] Assertion 5 'rows_upserted is 2' FAILED (got 3, expected 1).
      SQL: SELECT CASE WHEN rows_upserted = 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
           FROM dbo.SYS_DELTA_LOG ORDER BY log_id DESC OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROW ONLY;
```

All assertions in a test case run even if one fails — you always see the full picture
in a single run.

If the body SQL itself raises an exception (e.g. a syntax error or a constraint violation),
the test fails immediately with:

```
FAILED tests/test_sql_runner.py::test_sql_case[...]
AssertionError: Body SQL execution failed in 'TC07 - ...':
  INSERT INTO dbo.STAGING_FOLDERS ...
Error: ('23000', '[23000] ...')
```

